<h2>India's most valuable single market for seafood</h2>
<p>The United States takes more Indian frozen shrimp than any other single country — more than double the next-largest market by volume. Alongside that, it's a leading destination for basmati rice, psyllium husk (used widely in US dietary-fibre supplements), guar gum and red chilli.</p>

<h2>FDA compliance is the defining feature of this market</h2>
<p>More than any market we serve, US import compliance runs through a specific, well-documented regulatory system: FSMA (Food Safety Modernization Act), FSVP (Foreign Supplier Verification Program) on the importer's side, prior notice filing before arrival, and — for seafood — Seafood HACCP compliance at the processing facility.</p>
<p>None of this is exotic if you or your importer have done it before, but a first-time buyer should expect more documentation exchange than with most other markets. We're used to it and can move quickly through the requests, but the process itself takes longer than a Gulf or South Asian shipment.</p>

<h2>Import alerts are worth understanding before you order</h2>
<p>FDA maintains import alerts that can place specific products, or specific exporters with a history of violations, under automatic detention pending testing. This is a system-level risk on any US food import, not specific to us — but it's exactly why we recommend pre-shipment testing on products where the FDA has historically focused attention: spices (residue and Salmonella), groundnut and sesame (aflatoxin), and psyllium (purity and contamination). {{ site.inspectionStatement }}</p>

<h2>Transit is long — plan inventory accordingly</h2>
<p>At 25–35 days to the East Coast, US shipments need longer lead-time planning than almost any other market we serve. Build that into reorder timing, especially for products with seasonal demand peaks.</p>

